              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Grade range</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">-6 to 15 levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0-15 levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0-15 levels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">-6% to 40%</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Max user weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">350 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">350 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folds</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price and stock</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,899.99, sold out</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,199.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,599.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,699, in stock</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole F85.</strong> The closest Sole: the same running surface and 15.6-inch
            touchscreen, a folding frame, and $700 less than the TT8’s list price. It gives up decline,
            half a horsepower and 50 lb of rating. If you do not need decline and weigh well under
            350 lb, it is the sensible buy today; our <a href="/sole-f85-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F85 review</a> sets out its versions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole F80.</strong> $1,300 less than the TT8, with a 20-inch belt, a 3.25 HP motor
            and a 10.1-inch touchscreen. It suits runners of ordinary build who want Sole’s frame and
            motor cover without the TT8’s size; our <a href="/sole-f80-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F80 review</a> covers it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack X16.</strong> If decline is the reason you wanted the TT8, the X16
            declines to -6% and climbs to 40%, with the same 400 lb rating and 22 by 60-inch belt, for
            $800 more than the TT8’s list price. Most of its screen depends on a paid iFIT membership,
            and its parts cover is two years; our <a href="/nordictrack-x24-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack X24 review</a> covers the same machine with a larger screen. The X16 is sold by NordicTrack rather than on
            Amazon US, so the decline card on this page is the Freemotion i11.9, an older 30% incline
            trainer with decline that Amazon lists. The full Sole range is ranked in our <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best Sole treadmills</a> guide, and other heavy non-folding machines in our <a href="/best-non-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">non-folding roundup</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If you find one elsewhere</h3>
          <p>
            Dealers and second-hand sellers do have TT8s. Before you pay, ask which console it has,
            check the price against Sole’s $2,899.99 list, and get the warranty terms in writing. A
            dealer price above Sole’s list for a machine that Sole itself will sell you when stock
            returns is rarely worth paying. For a used TT8, our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> covers the belt, deck and motor checks, and the incline motors are worth running through
            their full range, decline included.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy the Sole TT8</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want decline from Sole.</strong> It is the only current Sole that
            goes downhill, and it does so on a heavy, stable, non-folding frame.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you are a heavy user or run high mileage.</strong> A 400 lb rating,
            4.0 HP motor, 3-inch rollers and a reversible deck are built for hard use, and the
            lifetime frame and motor cover backs them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the F85 instead</strong> if you need a treadmill now, want it to fold, or do not
            need decline. You keep the deck size and the screen and save $700.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere</strong> if you mainly walk, where the TT8 is more machine than you
            need, or if you want classes to control the treadmill, where iFIT-based NordicTrack
            machines are designed for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Whatever you buy, check the version.</strong> The current TT8’s warranty is shorter
            than the older one’s, so do not rely on a review or a listing that describes the LCD model.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Sole TT8</h2>
          <p>The TT8 is a good machine with a confusing history. These are the errors that cost buyers money or frustration.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting a review of the older version</h3>
              <p>Many TT8 reviews describe the 10.1-inch LCD model with a lifetime deck and two years of labour. The current touchscreen model has three years on the deck and one on labour. Check which version any source describes.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying over list to a reseller while Sole is sold out</h3>
              <p>Sole lists the TT8 at $2,899.99. A third-party price above that, for a machine Sole will sell when stock returns, is rarely worth paying unless you need it urgently and the warranty is confirmed in writing.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying it for walking</h3>
              <p>The TT8’s motor, rollers and 400 lb frame are built for hard running. If you mainly walk, a folding Sole or a cheaper machine does the job for far less.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting it does not fold</h3>
              <p>At 81 by 38 inches and 319 lb, the TT8 stays where it is installed. Measure the room, the doorway and the ceiling before you order, and plan the delivery route for a 367 lb box.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">The Sole TT8 is Sole’s heaviest, most capable treadmill</strong>: a 4.0 HP
            motor, a 22 by 60-inch deck, decline, a 400 lb rating and a lifetime frame and motor
            warranty, on a non-folding frame. It was sold out on Sole’s US store on 5 November 2026,
            and the current version’s warranty is shorter than older reviews describe.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you can wait, wait for Sole’s $2,899.99 list price. If you cannot, the <a href="/sole-f85-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Sole F85</a> gives you the same deck and screen for $700 less without decline, and the NordicTrack X16
            gives you more decline for more money.
          </p>
        </div>

